

///1ABORT
    Syntax:

    ABORT programname
    ABORT programname n

    ABORT immediately stops execution of  the  program  specified  by
    programname.  If  you  want to abort a program running on another
    console, include the  number  (n)  of  the  console  running  the
    program.

    Use CTRL-C to abort a program  running  on  the  current  virtual
    console,  and  the  ABORT command for programs running on another
    virtual console.  If you abort more than one program,  the  ABORT
    commands are executed in the order given.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    In  the  following  example, the  ABORT command stops the program
    TYPE which is displaying the contents of the file DOCUMENT.TXT on
    the screen.  Assume that the TYPE command was issued from virtual
    console 1, and is aborted from virtual console 3.  The drive and
    user number do not affect the ABORT command.

    A>TYPE DOCUMENT.TXT

    Dear Sir:
    The company is pleased to inform you tha...

    <switch virtual console screen>

    A>ABORT type 1
    A>

///1asm86
    Syntax:

    ASM86 filespec {$options}

    Purpose:

    ASM-86 assembles assembly language statements, producing  a  file
    in hexadecimal format, a print file and a symbol table file.  The
    assumed filetype of  source  and  included  files  is  A86.   The
    special  characters  X,  Y  and Z indicate output to the console,
    output to the printer,  and  zero  output,  respectively.  If  no
    format is specified, Digital Research format (FD) is assumed.

    ///2Examples
    Examples:

        A>ASM86 PROG
        A>ASM86 PROG $SZ PX
        A>ASM86 PROG $HB PY AC

    ///2Options
    Syntax:

    ASM86 filespec ($Ad Hd Pd Sd Fd)

    A   source file drive - .A86
    d = (logical drives A-P)

    H   hex file drive    - .H86
    d = (logical drives A-P, X,Y,Z)

    P   print file drive  - .LST
    d = (logical drives A-P, X,Y,Z)

    S   symbol file drive - .SYM
    d = (logical drives A-P, X,Y,Z)

    F   format of hex file- .H86
    d = (D=Digital Research, I=Intel)


///1BUFFERED
    You can switch the current (foreground) virtual console into  the
    background  by selecting a different virtual console for display.
    If the switched-out virtual console  is  in  buffered  mode,  any
    program output  to  that console is stored in a disk file.  Then,
    when the background virtual console is again  switched  into  the
    foreground,  any  output  stored  in the file is displayed on the
    monitor.

    The other  virtual  console mode is dynamic mode.  When a console
    is switched out and in dynamic mode,  output that scrolls off the
    screen is  not saved.  Use the VCMODE command  to switch consoles
    from one mode to the other.

///1CHSET
    Syntax:

    CHSET {d:}filename{.CMD}
    CHSET {d:}filename{.CMD} [field=setting{,field=setting,...}]
    CHSET [HELP]

    Explanation:

    CHSET is a utility that allows you to make changes in the command
    header  of a command file with a  .CMD filetype.  CHSET has three
    fields that you can display or change, corresponding to the three
    fields that CHSET modifies in a command header.  The three fields
    and their possible settings are:

    8087 - The  8087  field  has three possible settings, ON, OFF, or
    OPT  (optional).   Specify  ON  for programs that require an 8087
    processor  in  order  to run.  Choose OPT when you have a program
    that  can use an 8087 if it is present in your system, or emulate
    an  8087 if it is not present.  Specify OFF if you want to cancel
    a previous ON or OPT selection.

    SHARED - The SHARED field has two settings, ON or OFF.  Select ON
    when  you  want  to modify a program's command header so that the
    program  can  use  Concurrent  CP/M's  shared  code capabilities.
    Specify OFF if you want to cancel a previous ON selection.

    SUSPEND - The SUSPEND field has two options, ON or OFF.  When you
    select  ON,  CHSET  modifies  a  program's command header so that
    Concurrent  CP/M  suspends  that   program's  operation  when  it
    occupies a  switched-out virtual console.  OFF cancels a previous
    ON selection.

    Changing Field Settings

    You  can  change any or all of CHSET's fields in a single command
    line.  To  change  a setting for a field or fields, enter a CHSET
    command  with  a filespec and the fields you want to change.  For
    example, if you type

    B>chset calc.cmd [8087=opt,shared=on,suspend=on]

    CHSET  modifies  the command header of a program contained in the
    file CALC.CMD.

    Displaying Field Settings

    When  you  enter  a  CHSET  command  with a filespec and no field
    settings,  CHSET  displays the settings for the specified file or
    files.  For example, if you type

    B>chset erase.cmd

    CHSET responds with the following display:

    B:ERASE   .CMD settings are [8087=OFF,SHARED=OFF,SUSPEND=OFF]

    Modifying  a command header is not a trivial matter.  While CHSET
    cannot  harm  your  .CMD files, inappropriate use of it can cause
    your programs to behave in unpredictable ways.

    CHSET [HELP]

    If you type "help" in square brackets following the CHSET command
    keyword  in  a command line, CHSET responds with a display giving
    you  a  brief  explanation  of  CHSET  and  a list of examples of
    correct  command  line syntax.  You can also type CHSET without a
    command tail to summon CHSET's HELP display.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>chset taxaid.cmd [shared=on,8087=opt,suspend=off]

    The above command modifies the command header of a program called
    TAXAID so that it can use Concurrent CP/M's facilities for shared
    code and can either use an 8087 if it is present in the system or
    emulate an 8087 if it is not present.  The command also turns off
    the  SUSPEND  field for TAXAID, so that TAXAID can run when it is
    in  a  switched-out  virtual  console.  The inclusion of the .CMD
    filetype is optional.

    A>chset *

    The above command displays the current settings for every command
    file  in the current directory.  CHSET does not accept a filespec
    of *.* because it accepts only a blank filetype or filetype .CMD.

    B>chset ezesort [8087=on]

    The  above  command  modifies  the  command  header for a program
    called EZESORT so that it can use an installed 8087 processor.

    A>chset rectify.cmd

    The  above  command causes CHSET to respond with a display of the
    current settings of the fields in RECTIFY.CMD's command header.


///1CLS
    CLS clears the screen.

    CLS is a built-in shell command available at the prompt in both
    the native TMP and PC-MODE. It sends the terminal clear-screen
    sequence (ESC E) and does not require an external file.


///1cntrlchars

  Control Character                      Function

  CTRL-C    stops executing program  when  entered  at  the  system
            prompt or after CTRL-S.

  CTRL-E    forces  a  physical  carriage  return  without  sending
            command to CCP/M.

  CTRL-I   same as the TAB key.

  CTRL-H    delete character to the left of cursor.

  CTRL-J    Line feed.  Same effect as RETURN.

  CTRL-O    Suppresses console output.  Program continues running in
            the  background.   Press  RETURN to return to the system
            prompt.

  CTRL-M    same as RETURN.

  CTRL-P    echoes console output to the list device.

  CTRL-Q    restarts screen scrolling after a CTRL-S.

  CTRL-R    retypes the characters to the left of the cursor  on  a
            new line.

  CTRL-S    stops screen scrolling.

  CTRL-U    cancels current command line.

  CTRL-X    deletes all characters to the left of the cursor.

  CTRL-Z    string separator for PIP and ED; terminates console input
            when console is used as a source device with PIP.

///1COMMANDS

    Type ? at the shell prompt for a compact list of common commands.
    Type ? PIP (with a space) to run HELP for PIP. Topic lookup requires
    HELP.CMD and HELP.HLP; bare ? is built into the shell. A ? topic line
    may contain at most 125 characters including the question mark.

    PUT, GET and RDR redirect the console output and/or input of a
    program to or from a disk file. See HELP PUT, HELP GET and HELP RDR.

    SETDEF displays or changes the command drive search list for this
    console. The default is the current drive, followed by the fixed
    system fallback. See HELP SETDEF for drive-list syntax and examples.

    Concurrent CP/M command line syntax:

    A> <command> {command tail} <RETURN>

    Concurrent CP/M  accepts the command lines you type following the
    system prompt.  The command keyword identifies the system command
    or program to be executed.  The optional command tail can consist
    of  a  filespec or various command parameters, depending upon the
    specific command.  To  complete  the command, press the RETURN or
    ENTER key.  File  specifications in command lines are composed of
    the following parts:

    {d:}filename{.typ}{;password}

    d:              is an optional drive specifier
    filename        is the 1- to 8-character file name
    .typ            is an optional file type
    ;password       is an optional 1- to 8-character password

///1CONFIG
    Syntax:

    CONFIG

    Purpose:


    Concurrent CP/M  supports  three  parallel I/O  printers  (0, 1
     2  )  and two  serial I/O  printers (3, 4).  The CONFIG command
    lets  you  specify  the  parameters of   the   serial   printers
    interactively or in a command line.

    Interactive Mode:

    When you enter:

    A>config

    the CONFIG menu appears.  If there is  only one serial  printer
    connected, only the  Printer  3 column appears; if you have two
    serial printers  a  column  for Printer 4  apppears.  This menu
    shows the four sets  of  parameters  for  printers 3 and 4, odd
    numbered keys for printer 3 and even-numbered keys for printer 4.

    The values shown when you invoke CONFIG are the current values;
    change  them by pressing the appropriate keys, with each stroke
    incrementing the appropriate value.  The F9 and F10 keys return
    you to command level.

    BAUD RATE is the speed at which the computer sends data  to its
    printer.  Refer  to  your printer's  technical  manual  for the
    correct baud rate to program.

    WORD LENGTH  indicates  the number of data  bits  in  a  single
    transmitted  character.  This  value is  almost always  7 or 8.
    Again, consult  the printer's documentation  to  determine  the
    correct value.

    PARITY monitors characters  to see  if they are transmitted and
    received correctly.  Often both  the  printer  and computer can
    enable or disable parity checking.  Refer to the printer manual
    for specific details relevant to your printer.

    STOP BITS  indicate  the end of a  transmitted  character.  See
    your printer's manual for the proper value; 1 is commonly used.

    Command Line Mode:

    To define all serial port parameters  in a single command line,
    use this syntax:

    CONFIG PORT# BAUDRATE WORDLENGTH PARITY STOPBITS

    In this  format, PORT# is either P3, indicating printer 3,  or
    P4, indicating printer 4.

    BAUDRATE  is  10, 150, 300, 600, 1200, 2400,  4800,  or  9600,
    indicating the transmisstion speed in baud (bits-per-second).

    WORDLENGTH is 5, 6, 7, or 8, specifying the number of data bits
    for a transmitted character.

    STOPBITS is either 1 or 2, adding that number of stop bits to the
    end of a transmitted character.

    ///2EXAMPLE

    A typical CONFIG command line looks like this:

    A>config p3 9600 7 even 1

    This command line sets serial printer P3 to 9600 baud, with seven
    data bits, even parity and 1 stop bit.

    If you make an mistake in a CONFIG command line, the command line
    is returned with a question mark under the error, and  a list  of
    valid parameters follows.

    If you have tried to configure a serial port on a  system without
    such ports, CONFIG returns this error message:

    No Serial communication options installed

    Then control returns to system command level.

///1conventions

               COMMAND CONVENTIONS

  The following special symbols define command syntax.

  {}   surrounds an optional item.
  |    separates alternative items in a command line.
  <cr> indicates a carriage return.
  ^    indicates the Control Key.
  n    substitute a number for n.
  s    substitute a string (group) of characters for s.
  o    substitute an option or option list for o.
  []   type square brackets to enclose an option list.
  ()   type parens to enclose a range of options within an option list.
  RW   Read-Write attribute - opposite of RO
  RO   Read-Only attribute - opposite of RW
  SYS  System attribute - opposite of DIR
  DIR  Directory attribute - opposite of SYS
   ... preceding element can be repeated as many times as desired.
  *    wildcard: replaces all or part of a filename and/or filetype.
  ?    wildcard: replaces any single character
       in the same position of a filename and/or filetype.

///1DATE
  DATE.CMD is the native CP/M command on CCP/M and CDOS. On CDOS,
  DATE.EXE is the separate DOS interface; use an explicit extension to select it.
  Short years 00..77 mean 2000..2077; 78..99 mean 1978..1999.

    Syntax:

    DATE
    DATE CONTINUOUS
    DATE date/time-specification
    DATE SET

    Explanation:

    The DATE  utility  allows  you  to set  the system  clock  to the
    correct date and time. It also allows you to display the date and
    time on the current virtual console.

    The date is  represented in  conventional  month-day-year  format
    MM/DD/YY  while the  time is represented in 24-hour clock format:
    00:00:00  to  11:59:59  indicates  AM,  and  12:00:00 to 23:59:59
    indicates PM.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>DATE

    Displays the current date and time.

    A>DATE C

    Displays the date and time continuously.

    A>DATE 11/28/83 10:35:45

    Sets the date and time as specified.

    A>DATE SET

    Prompts you for date and time entries.


///1dcomp
  Syntax:

  DCOMP drive1: {drive2:} {[1]} {[8]|[9]}

  Purpose:

      Compare raw floppy sectors, including boot and unused areas.
  CP/M and DOS/FAT media are supported; no filesystem conversion occurs.
  Geometry is 40 cylinders, 512-byte sectors, one/two heads, eight/nine
  sectors per head: 160/320K or 180/360K.

  drive1:   First installed floppy, A: or B: (required).
  drive2:   Second floppy; defaults to the current drive.
  [1]       Head zero only. Use for known single-sided media on native XIOS.
  [8]/[9]   Select sectors per track. Conflicting options are rejected.

      Without [8]/[9], validate a FAT12 BPB and probe sector nine. Success
  proves nine; failed probing alone never proves eight. Unknown eight-sector
  media needs [8]. A known BPB contradicting the selection is rejected.
  Extended CDOS also detects 720K, 1.2M and 1.44M DOS FAT12 BPBs;
  these use 80 cylinders. No capacity option is needed.
  [9] does not ignore unreadable sectors. DCOMP checks both geometries.

      DCOMP is read-only and reports differences by track and side.
  A geometry conflict stops comparison without reporting equality.
  Same drive twice prompts for swaps. Y processes another pair; N exits.
  Closing ] is optional; for example B:[1] [8] or A: B:[9.

      CP/M-86 1.1 uses BIOS; CCP/M-86 3.1 and CDOS 4.1 use native XIOS.
  CCP/M nine-sector operation requires the updated XIOS raw services.
  Older CCP/M retains the eight-sector path; unknown versions are rejected.
  Use a local foreground console and close files first on native systems.
  Ctrl-C releases locks and restores the original disk parameter vector.

  Examples:
      A>DCOMP A: B: [9]
      A>DCOMP A: A: [1] [8]


///1dcopy
  Syntax:

  DCOPY drive1: {drive2:} {[1]} {[8]|[9]}

  Purpose:

      Copy and verify raw floppy sectors, including boot and unused areas.
  CP/M and DOS/FAT media are supported; no filesystem conversion occurs.
  Geometry is 40 cylinders, 512-byte sectors, one/two heads, eight/nine
  sectors per head: 160/320K or 180/360K.

  drive1:   Source installed floppy, A: or B: (required).
  drive2:   Target floppy; defaults to the current drive.
  [1]       Head zero only. Use for known single-sided media on native XIOS.
  [8]/[9]   Select sectors per track. Conflicting options are rejected.

      Without [8]/[9], validate a FAT12 BPB and probe sector nine. Success
  proves nine; failed probing alone never proves eight. Unknown eight-sector
  media needs [8]. A known BPB contradicting the selection is rejected.
  Extended CDOS also detects 720K, 1.2M and 1.44M DOS FAT12 BPBs;
  these use 80 cylinders. No capacity option is needed.
  [9] does not ignore unreadable sectors. DCOMP checks both geometries.

      DCOPY formats all target tracks to source geometry before writing and
  verifying. Every previous target file is overwritten.
  Same drive twice prompts for swaps. Y processes another pair; N exits.
  Closing ] is optional; for example B:[1] [8] or A: B:[9.

      CP/M-86 1.1 uses BIOS; CCP/M-86 3.1 and CDOS 4.1 use native XIOS.
  CCP/M nine-sector operation requires the updated XIOS raw services.
  Older CCP/M retains the eight-sector path; unknown versions are rejected.
  Use a local foreground console and close files first on native systems.
  Ctrl-C releases locks and restores the original disk parameter vector.

  Examples:
      A>DCOPY A: B: [9]
      A>DCOPY A: A: [1] [8]


///1DDT86
    Syntax:

    DDT86 {filespec}

    Explanation:

    DDT86  aids debugging of 8086 and 8088 programs.  DDT86 assumes a
    default  filetype  of  CMD.   If  a  file  specification  is  not
    included,  DDT86  is  loaded  into  User  Memory  without  a test
    program. The test program is then loaded using the E command.  If
    the  optional file specification is included in the command, both
    DDT86 and the test program file specified by filespec are  loaded
    into  memory. DDT86  does  not support passwords.  To exit DDT86,
    press CTRL-C.

    Do not use G, T or U until a test program has been loaded.
    Use E to load a CMD program when starting DDT86 without a filename.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>DDT86
    A>DDT86 PROGRAM1
    A>DDT86 PROGRAM2.CMD
    A>DDT86 B:PROGRAM3.CMD

    DDT86 Command Examples:

    -D
    -L1008:0,4F
    -SW23a
    -DW50:0
    -F100,200,FF
    -A10C
    -Rprog.h86

    ///2COMMANDS
    DDT86 Command Summary:

    The command character can be followed by one or  more  arguments.
    Separate  arguments  from  one  another  by  commas or spaces; no
    spaces are allowed between the command character  and  the  first
    argument.

    As             (Assemble)      Enter Assembly Language Statements
    Bs,f,s1        (Block Compare) Compare Blocks of Memory
    D{W}{s{,f}}    (Display)       Display Memory in Hexadecimal and ASCII
    Efilespec      (Execution)     Load Program for Execution
    Fs,f,bc        (Fill)          Fill Memory Block with a byte constant
    FWs,f,wc       (Fill Word)     Fill Memory Block with a word constant
    G{s}{,b1{,b2}} (Go)            Begin Execution with optional breakpoints
    Hwc1,wc2       (Hex)           Hexadecimal Sum and Difference
    Icommand tail  (Input)         Set Up Input Command Line
    L{s{,f}}       (List)          List Memory in Mnemonic Form
    Ms,f,d         (Move)          Move Memory Block
    QI{W}n                         Read From I/O Port
    QO{W}n,v                       Write To I/O Port
    Rfilespec      (Read)          Read Disk File into Memory
    S{W}s          (Set)           Set Memory Values
    SRs,f,<string>                 Search For String
    T{n}           (Trace)         Trace Program Execution
    TS{n}          (Trace)         Trace and Show All Registers
    U{n}           (Untrace)       Monitor execution without Trace
    US{n}          (Untrace)       Monitor and Show all Registers
    V              (Verify)        Show Memory Layout after Disk Read
    Wfilespec      (Write)         Write Content of Block to Disk
    X{r}           (Examine)       Examine and Modify CPU Registers


    DDT-86 Command Parameters
    -------------------------
    Parameter   Replace With

    bc          byte constant
    b1          breakpoint one
    b2          breakpoint two
    d           destination for data
    f           final address
    n           number of instructions
                to execute
    r           register or flag name
    s           starting address
    W           word 16-bit
    wc          word constant

    ///3PARAMETERS
    DDT-86 Command Parameters

    Parameter   Replace With

    bc          byte constant
    b1          breakpoint one
    b2          breakpoint two
    d           destination for data
    f           final address
    n           number of instructions
                to execute
    r           register or flag name
    s           starting address
    W           word 16-bit
    wc          word constant


///1DFORM
  Syntax:

  DFORM drive: { [options] ... }

  Purpose:

      DFORM formats one floppy diskette using the same track layout,
  boot sector and loader as DSKMAINT. The target drive is required and
  must be an installed A: or B: floppy. All existing contents are replaced.
  The command displays the selected capacity and verification setting,
  and requires Y confirmation before formatting. N or Ctrl-C cancels.

  Options:

  1         Single-sided, 160K: 40 cylinders, eight 512-byte sectors.
  2         Double-sided, 320K: the default if neither 1 nor 2 is given.
  V         Verify every track after formatting and writing the boot track.
            Without V, no verification pass is performed.

      Combine options in one bracket group or use separate groups.
  The closing ] is optional. Drive letters and options are case-insensitive.
  Conflicting 1/2 options, unknown options and other incorrect arguments
  display usage and exit. An empty command line also displays usage.

      This build supports CP/M-86 1.1 using the IBM PC ROM BIOS and
  Concurrent CP/M-86 3.1 using native XIOS. Nine-sector 180K/360K formats
  are not supported. The formatted disk contains DSKMAINT's boot code
  and the loader for the detected OS; copy CPM.SYS or CCPM.SYS to the
  disk with PIP's [R] option to complete a boot disk.

  Examples:

      A>DFORM A:
      A>DFORM B:[1V]
      A>DFORM A: [V
      A>DFORM A: [1] [V]
      A>DFORM A: [1] [V

///1DIR
    Syntax:

    DIR
    DIR d:
    DIR [Gn]
    DIR [S]
    DIR filespec
    DIR filespec [Gn]
    DIR filespec [S]
    DIR filespec [P]

    Explanation:

    Displays the names of non-system (DIR) files in the directory  of
    an  on-line  diskette.     Use DIR [S] to  display  the  names of
    SYSTEM files only; on FAT this includes HIDDEN. Use DIR [Gn]
    to display non-system files in User n.

    [P] pauses after 23 screen rows. It works with both the resident DIR
    and DIR.CMD, and can be combined with [S] or [Gn]. The prompt is the
    native PCTMP PAUSE message: Press any key to continue...
    Press a key to display the next page, or Ctrl-C to stop the listing.
    Without [P], output is continuous. Paging resets for each command.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>DIR
    A>DIR B:
    A>DIR C:MYFILE.DAT
    A>DIR *.CMD
    A>DIR A*.A86
    A>DIR PROG???.H86
    A>DIR PROGRAM.*
    A>DIR [S]
    A>DIR B:UTILITY.CMD [S]
    A>DIR A: B: [P]
    A>DIR.CMD A: B: [P]

    ///2CMD
    DIR.CMD {filespec} {[S]} {[Gn]} {[P]}

    Explicit DIR.CMD invokes the shared standalone native command.
    [S] lists system files only; on CDOS FAT this includes hidden files.
    [Gn] selects CP/M user n (0..15). [P] pauses after 23 screen rows,
    using Press any key to continue...; Ctrl-C stops the listing.
    Without [P], output is continuous. Options can be combined.

    A>DIR.CMD B:*.CMD [S] [P]
    A>DIR.CMD A: B: [P]

    Interactive DIR and BATCH may use their own resident implementations;
    their additional switches are documented in the parent DIR topic.


///1DSKMAINT
    DSKMAINT.CMD is on the bootable maintenance disk (ccpmmaint.img),
    alongside HDMAINT and INITDIR.

    Syntax:

    DSKMAINT

    Explanation:

    DSKMAINT is a  multi-function  menu-driven command  that performs
    these diskette maintenance operations: formatting new or reusable
    diskettes,  copying  one  diskette's  contents  to  another,  and
    verifying the integrity of the data on diskettes.

    Single and Double-Sided Diskettes:

    DSKMAINT can only  copy  between  two diskettes of the same type:
    between two  single-sided  diskettes  or between two double-sided
    diskettes.  Look at the  box of  diskettes  or at the label on an
    individual diskette to determine if it is single or double-sided.

    To make a double-sided copy  of a single-sided diskette, format a
    double-sided diskette, then use PIP to copy files between the two
    diskettes.

    Before you invoke  DSKMAINT, be sure your current virtual console
    is in Dynamic mode.  You can  set the console to  Dynamic mode by
    using the VCMODE command.

///1DYNAMIC
    You can switch the current (foreground) virtual console into  the
    background  by selecting a different virtual console for display.
    If the switched-out virtual console is in  dynamic mode, you will
    not  see  any  program  output that may  have  scrolled  off that
    console while you were switched away.

    If the console is  in  buffered mode, this  data  is  stored in a
    temporary disk file and displayed when you switch back.

    Use the  VCMODE  command  to switch  a  console  from  dynamic to
    buffered mode if you anticipate  a  program  outputting more than
    one screen of data, and you do not wish to miss it.

///1DOS8087
    Syntax:

    DOS8087
    DOS8087 {ON | OFF}

    Explanation:

    DOS8087 enables or disables 8087 numeric coprocessor support for DOS
    applications running on the current virtual console.

    When entered without an argument, DOS8087 displays the current 8087 state.
    Setting DOS8087 ON requires an 8087 math coprocessor to be installed.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

        A>DOS8087          ; displays 8087 = ON or OFF
        A>DOS8087 ON       ; enables 8087 coprocessor support
        A>DOS8087 OFF      ; disables 8087 coprocessor support

///1DOSMEM
    Syntax:

    DOSMEM
    DOSMEM {size_in_kib}

    Explanation:

    DOSMEM displays or sets the default memory partition size (in KiB)
    allocated for DOS (.COM and .EXE) processes running on the current
    virtual console.

    When entered without an argument, DOSMEM displays the current allocation.
    The default is 128 KiB.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

        A>DOSMEM           ; displays DOS Memory Allocation (e.g. 128K)
        A>DOSMEM 256       ; sets DOS process memory size to 256 KiB
        A>DOSMEM 64        ; sets DOS process memory size to 64 KiB

///1DOSUSPND
    Syntax:

    DOSUSPND
    DOSUSPND {ON | OFF}

    Explanation:

    DOSUSPND controls whether background DOS processes continue executing
    or are suspended when switching away from their virtual console.

    When entered without an argument, DOSUSPND displays the current state.
    Setting DOSUSPND ON suspends background DOS tasks; OFF allows them to
    continue running in the background.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

        A>DOSUSPND         ; displays Suspend = ON or OFF
        A>DOSUSPND ON      ; suspends DOS tasks when switched out
        A>DOSUSPND OFF     ; keeps DOS tasks running in background

///1ED
    Syntax:

    ED input-filespec {d:|output-filespec}

    Explanation:

    ED is the CP/M character file editor.  You can rename
    or redirect the  new  version  of  the  file  by  specifying  the
    destination drive or destination filespec.

    ///2COMMANDS
    ED Command Summary:

    Note: CP points to the current character in the edit buffer.

    nA            append n lines from original file to memory buffer.
    0A            append file until buffer is one-half full
    #A            append file until buffer is full (or end of file)
    B, -B         move CP to the beginning (B) or bottom (-B) of buffer
    nC, -nC       move CP n characters forward (C) or back (-C) through buffer
    nD, -nD       delete n characters before (-D) or from (D) the CP
    E             save new file and return to the command prompt
    Fstring^Z     find character string
    H             save new file, re-edit using new file as the original file
    I<RETURN>     enter insert mode
    Istring^Z     insert string at CP
    Jsearch_str^Zins_str^Zdel_to_str     juxtapose strings
    nK, -nK       delete (kill) n lines from the CP
    nL, -nL       move CP n lines
    nMcommand     execute command n times
    n, -n         move CP n lines and display that line
    n:            move to line n
    :ncommand     execute command through line n
    Nstring       extended find string
    O             return to original file
    nP, -nP       move CP n lines forward and display n lines at console
    Q             abandon new file, return to the command prompt
    R             read X$$$$$$$.LIB file into buffer
    Rfilespec^Z   read filespec into buffer
    Sdelete string^Zinsert string^Z     substitute string
    nT, -nT       type n lines
    U, -U         upper-case translation
    V, -V         line numbering on/off
    0V            display free buffer space
    nW            write n lines to new file
    0W            write until buffer is half empty
    nX            write or append n lines to X$$$$$$$.LIB
    nXfilespec^Z  write n lines to filespec or append if previous X
                  command applied to the same file
    0X            delete file X$$$$$$$.LIB
    0Xfilespec^Z  delete filespec
    nZ            wait n seconds

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>ED TEST.DAT
    A>ED TEST.DAT B:
    A>ED TEST.DAT TEST2.DAT
    A>ED TEST.DAT B:TEST2.DAT

    Command Examples:

    :#a
    :p
    :4c0tt
    :e
    :5K
    :B5T
    :Rmyfile.txt


///1ERA
    Syntax:

    ERA filespec

    Explanation:

    ERA erases a file or group of files.  ERA accepts ambiguous  file
    specifications.  The ERAQ command works like ERA, but prompts you
    before performing the erasure.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>ERA DRAFT.BAK
    A>ERA B:LETTER.DAT
    A>ERA C:LETTER.*
    A>ERA D:*.BAK
    A>ERA B:*.*

///1ERAQ
    Syntax:

    ERAQ filespec
    ERAQ filespec [XFCB]

    Explanation:

    ERAQ erases a file or group of files, prompting you  before  each
    deletion.  ERAQ accepts ambiguous filenames.    The [XFCB] option
    erases only the extra directory information for passwords.

    The ERA command works as ERAQ  does,  but  does  not  prompt  you
    before performing the erasure.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    1A>ERAQ B:*.CMD

    B:ABORT      CMD ?y
    B:FUNCTION   CMD ?n
    B:SDIR       CMD ?y
    B:PIP        CMD ?y
    1A>

    In the example, the user instructs ERAQ to delete all  the  files
    except C:FUNCTION.CMD.

    2B>ERAQ myfile.dat [XFCB]

    The command line above erases only the password information for the
    file myfile.dat.  First use SET to turn off password protection.

///1FILESPEC
    Concurrent CP/M  identifies   every  file   by  its  unique  file
    specification.  The  term  "filespec" is an abbreviation for file
    specification. A filespec can have four parts:

                      d:filename.typ;password

    d:         represents the optional drive specification, which can
               range from A through P, followed by a colon.

    filename   represents the required filename, which can be 1 to  8
               alphabetic or numeric characters.

    .typ       represents the optional filetype,  which can be 1 to 3
               alphabetic or numeric characters preceded by a period.

    ;password  represents the optional password,  which can be 1 to 8
               alphabetic  or  numeric  characters,   preceded  by  a
               semicolon.

    Valid combinations of the elements of a  file  specification  are
    shown below:

      filename
      d:filename
      filename.typ
      d:filename.typ
      filename;password
      d:filename;password
      filename.typ;password
      d:filename.typ;password

    Certain Concurrent CP/M commands select and process several files
    if  "wildcard" characters appear  in  the  filename  or filetype.
    The two wildcard characters are  ?, which matches a single letter
    in the same  position, and *, which matches any character at that
    position  and  any  other characters remaining in the filename or
    filetype.

    ///2CONVENTIONS

               COMMAND CONVENTIONS

  The following special symbols define command syntax.

  {}   surrounds an optional item.
  |    separates alternative items in a command line.
  <cr> indicates a carriage return.
  ^    indicates the Control Key.
  n    substitute a number for n.
  s    substitute a string (group) of characters for s.
  o    substitute an option or option list for o.
  []   type square brackets to enclose an option list.
  ()   type parens to enclose a range of options within an option list.
  RW   Read-Write attribute - opposite of RO
  RO   Read-Only attribute - opposite of RW
  SYS  System attribute - opposite of DIR
  DIR  Directory attribute - opposite of SYS
   ... preceding element can be repeated as many times as desired.
  *    wildcard: replaces all or part of a filename and/or filetype.
  ?    wildcard: replaces any single character
       in the same position of a filename and/or filetype.

///1FUNCTION
    Syntax:

    FUNCTION [filename.]
    FUNCTION [filename.typ]

    Purpose:

    FUNCTION specifies the operation of the 50 programmable  function
    keys (PFKs).  FUNCTION  operates  in  two modes, specified by the
    command tail entered with  FUNCTION.  If you  supply  no  command
    tail  FUNCTION  displays  menus  to  select from. If you  specify
    a filespec, FUNCTION takes its input from this file.

    FUNCTION programs  function  keys on a per-virtual console basis.
    PFK assignments you make in one  virtual console affect only that
    virtual console.

    Concurrent CP/M  boots  with  commands  programmed  into  the ten
    F1-F10  function  keys  at  the  left  of  the   keyboard.  These
    commands are:

    F1:  DIR\20A:\0D
    F2:  DIR\20B:\0D
    F3:  SDIR\20A:\0D
    F4:  SDIR\20B:\0D
    F5:  SHOW\20A:\0D
    F6:  SHOW\20B:\0D
    F7:  DSKMAINT\0D
    F8:  PIP\0D
    F9:  FUNCTION\0D
    F10: CONFIG\0D

    The other 40 keys, SHIFT F1-F10, ALT F1-F10, CTRL F1-F10, and the
    numeric  keypad are not pre-programmed.  FUNCTION will  allow you
    to program up to twenty characters in the first ten function keys
    (F1-F10) and up to four characters in all of the others.

    The \20 in the commands above indicates a space.  It is generated
    automatically when you enter a space into a command.

    The commands terminated with \0D  include  carriage  returns.
    The  commands  without  \0D  can  take  further specifications or
    parameters you supply.  Terminate them with a carriage return and
    Concurrent CP/M carries out the command.

    Using The FUNCTION Menu:

    When  you  first  call  FUNCTION the menu appears. The string  of
    Concurrent  CP/M  commands  and  symbols following "F-number:" is
    the programmed function.  Press  any  function key  to  go to the
    menu containing it and change the programmed value.  If you  make
    a mistake, just backspace over it to erase it.  You can terminate
    the string with \00 or CTRL-@ and return to the main menu.

    If you  want  to  alter  or inspect the other  PFKs,  press  ESC.
    Pressing ESC moves you  from  menu  to  menu of PFKs.  Entering a
    carriage return returns you to the system command level.

    Non-Printing Characters:

    You  can  specify  absolute  hexadecimal values so  all  possible
    control  characters and 8-bit character codes can be used.  Refer
    to  the  Concurrent  CP/M  User's  Guide  for  a  description  of
    programming hexadecimal codes into the PFKs.

    Saving Function Key Definitions:

    You can use the SETUP program to save  the function keys you have
    entered, so that every  time  you  boot Concurrent CP/M they will
    come up in your configuration.

    Using FUNCTION With a Command File:

    If you enter a  filespec  with  FUNCTION,  Concurrent  CP/M opens
    this  file  and  programs  the  function  keys with these command
    strings.  These files must  contain  a  separate  line  for  each
    function  key.  Examine the DATA.PFK file to learn how such files
    are constructed; use a text editor like ED  to  create  your  own
    customized version.  Refer to the FUNCTION command description in
    the  Concurrent  CP/M  User's  Guide  for  further details.

    Once you have  completed  writing  your  command file and checked
    it  for  errors,  you  can return to the system command level and
    call this command file to program the specified  keys.   If  your
    file is named PFK.PFK, for example, give the command:

    A>FUNCTION PFK.PFK

    FUNCTION reads the specified file, programs the keys accordingly,
    and returns to system command level.

    See Section 9 of the Concurrent CP/M users guide for further
    information about FUNCTION.

///1gencmd
    Syntax:

    GENCMD filespec {options}

    Purpose:

    Converts hexadecimal object file (filetype  assumed  to  be .H86)
    into  executable  file  (of  type .CMD). Switches controlling the
    type of memory model and group addresses can  be  included.   All
    values are hexadecimal and are paragraph values.

    ///2Examples
    Examples:

        A>GENCMD PROG
        A>GENCMD PROG1 8080
        A>GENCMD PROG2 DATA[M20]
        A>GENCMD PROG3 DATA[B4C,M260,XFFF]
        A>GENCMD PROG4 CODE[A40] DATA[M30]

    ///2options
    Syntax:

    GENCMD filespec  {8080
                      CODE[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                      DATA[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                     STACK[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]
                     EXTRA[An,Bn,Mn,Xn]}

       A = Absolute memory location
       B = Beginning address of group in .H86 file
       M = Minimum memory required
       X = Maximum memory required

    All values represented by n are hexadecimal paragraph addresses.


///1GET
    Syntax:

    GET [infile{,E|EI}] command-line

    Explanation:

    GET feeds the console input of a program from a text file instead
    of the keyboard.  The infile is read in 128-byte records until a
    CTRL-Z or the end of the file.  The line read at that point is
    empty, which ends programs such as PIP; any later input comes from
    the keyboard.  The program and any programs it starts on this
    virtual console receive the characters; other consoles are not
    affected.

    E or EI echoes the characters supplied from the file to the screen.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected; use
    the program forms such as SDIR.CMD or TYPE.CMD.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>GET [SCRIPT.IN] ED MYFILE.TXT
    A>GET [CMDS.DAT] PIP
    A>GET [BATCH.IN,E] DDT86 PROG.CMD

    See also: PUT, RDR

///1HDMAINT
HDMAINT

The shared HDMAINT 1.1N supports CP/M-86 and Concurrent CP/M-86. It is a
menu-driven hard-disk maintenance program: use the displayed function keys.
It creates/changes CP/M partitions and volumes, deletes volumes, selects the
active boot partition, scans for defects and controls write verification.
It can enlarge an existing volume's directory where that option is available.
Only meaningful operations appear in the menus. Partition/volume operations
can replace existing contents; read each confirmation before proceeding.

On CCP/M, use a local dynamic console (VCMODE) and terminate other programs
and open files before starting. The CP/M single-user path does not require
VCMODE. The CDOS HDMAINT program is a different implementation and is not
covered by this shared command topic.


///1HELP
  Syntax:

     HELP {topic} {subtopic1 ... subtopic8} {[NOPAGE|LIST]}

  Explanation:

  HELP  displays  a  list  of  topics   and   provides   summarized
  information for system commands.

  HELP topic displays information about that topic.
  HELP topic subtopic displays information about that subtopic.

  One or two letters is enough to identify the topics.  After  HELP
  displays   information   for   your  topic,   it   displays   the
  special  prompt  HELP> on your screen,  followed  by  a  list  of
  subtopics.

   -  Enter ? to display list of main topics.
   -  Enter a period and subtopic name to access subtopics.
   -  Enter a period to redisplay what you just read.
   -  Press the RETURN key to return to the system command prompt.
   -  [NOPAGE] doesn't stop at 24 lines; console display keeps scrolling.
   -  [LIST] removes extra lines between headings; use with CTRL-P to print.
   -  Press any key to exit a display and return to the HELP> prompt.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

       A>HELP
       A>HELP DATE
       A>HELP DIR
       A>HELP>.EXAMPLES
       A>HELP DIR EXAMPLES
       HELP>SET
       HELP>.PASSWORD
       HELP>SET PASSWORD
       HELP>.
       HELP><cr>


///1INITDIR
    Syntax:

    INITDIR d:

    Explanation:

    INITDIR  initializes   a   directory  to   allow  time  and  date
    stamping on a diskette or hard disk.  Attempting to  enable  time
    and date stamping on a directory that has not been reformatted by
    INITDIR  will  result in an error message.  INITDIR only works on
    disks that have already been formatted according to your computer
    manufacturer's instructions.

    ///2EXAMPLE
    Example:

    A>INITDIR C:

    INITDIR WILL ACTIVATE TIME STAMPS FOR SPECIFIED DRIVE
    Do you want to re-format the directory on drive: C (Y/N)?

    Answer with a "Y" to continue.  If the specified disk was already
    formatted for time and date stamps, INITDIR displays the message:

    Directory already re-formatted
    Do you want to recover time/date space (Y/N)?

    If your directory  is  full and  you previously  enabled time and
    date stamps, you can use INITDIR to free up the  directory space.
    If INITDIR  finds  time and  date  stamps  in  the  directory, it
    displays the following message:

    Do you want the existing time and date stamps cleared (Y/N)?

///1PIP (copy)
  Syntax:

              DESTINATION              SOURCE

    PIP d:{Gn} | filespec{[Gn]} = filespec{[o]},... | d:{[o]}

  Explanation:

  The file copy program  PIP  copies  files,  combines  files,  and
  transfers  files  between  disks,  printers,  consoles,  or other
  devices attached to your computer.  The  first  filespec  is  the
  destination. The  second filespec is the source.  Use two or more
  source filespecs separated by commas to combine two or more files
  into  one file.  [o] is any combination of the available options.
  The [Gn] option in the destination filespec  tells  PIP  to  copy
  your file to that user number.

  PIP with no command tail displays an *  prompt  and  awaits  your
  series  of  commands,  entered  and processed one line at a time.
  The source or destination can be any available logical device.

  ///2Examples

  COPY A FILE FROM ONE DISK TO ANOTHER

       A>PIP b:=a:draft.txt
       A>PIP b:draft.txt = a:

       B3>PIP myfile.dat=A:[G9]
       A9>PIP B:[G3]=myfile.dat

  COPY A FILE AND RENAME IT

       A5>PIP newdraft.txt=oldraft.txt
       C8>PIP b:newdraft.txt=a:oldraft.txt

  COPY MULTIPLE FILES

       A>PIP b:=draft.*
       A>PIP b:=*.*
       B>PIP b:=c:*.*
       C>PIP b:=*.txt[g5]
       C>PIP a:=*.cmd[wr]
       B>PIP a:[g3]=c:*.*

  COMBINE MULTIPLE FILES

       A>PIP b:new.dat=file1.dat,file2.dat

  COPY, RENAME AND PLACE IN USER 1

       A>pip newdraft.txt[g1]=oldraft.txt

  COPY, RENAME AND GET FROM USER 1

       A>PIP newdraft.txt=oldraft.txt[g1]

  COPY TO/FROM LOGICAL DEVICES

       A>PIP b:funfile.sue=con:
       A>PIP lst:=con:
       A>PIP lst:=b:draft.txt[t8]
       A>PIP prn:=b:draft.txt

  ///2options

  PIP OPTIONS

  A    Archive. Copy only files that have been  changed  since  the
        last copy.
  C    Confirm. PIP prompts for confirmation before each file copy.
  Dn   Delete any characters past column n.
  E    Echo transfer to console.
  F    Filter form-feeds from source data.
  Gn   Get from or go to user n.
  H    Test for valid Hex format.
  I    Ignore :00 Hex data records and test for valid Hex format.
  K    Kill display of filespecs on console.
  L    Translate upper case to lower case.
  N    Number output lines
  O    Object file transfer, ^Z ignored.
  Pn   Set page length to n.  (default n=60)
  Qs^Z Quit copying from source at string s.
  R    Read files that have been set to SYStem.
  Ss^Z Start copying from the source at the string s.
  Tn   Expand tabs to n spaces.
  U    Translate lower case to upper case.
  V    Verify that data has been written correctly.
  W    Write over Read Only files without console query.
  Z    Zero the parity bit.

  [A] and [C] apply to explicit filenames as well as wildcards.

  With [H] or [I], the first printable character of each HEX
  record must be a colon (:).

  All  options  except  C,G,K,O,R,V  and  W  force  an  ASCII  file
  transfer, character by character, terminated by a ^Z.


///1PRINT
    Syntax:

    PRINT filename{[options]}{,filename{[options],...}

    Explanation:

    The  PRINT  utility  allows  you to send the files you enter on a
    PRINT command line to the printer.

    You must enter at least one filespec on a PRINT command line. You
    can enter as many filespecs as will fit on Concurrent CP/M's 128-
    character  command  line.   PRINT  allows  you  to  use  wildcard
    filespecs.   On a PRINT command line, separate filespecs with one
    space or a comma.

    PRINT Options

    o S (SYSTEM) - The S option  allows you to  print a file that has
      the SYS attribute.

    o Tn (TAB) - n is  a  number  of character spaces.   The T option
      allows you to expand or compress tab characters. PRINT replaces
      each  tab in a file  with  enough  spaces  to position the next
      character in a column that is divisible by n.   PRINT's default
      setting for tab expansion is eight spaces.

    o Gn (GET FROM) - n is a user number.  The G option allows you to
      print a file that is not in the current user number.

    Enter options in square brackets following the filespec for which
    you want to exercise the option.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    To print more than one copy of a file, repeat the file's filespec
    on the PRINT command line for each additional copy that you want.

    4B>print murphy.txt[g3],murphy.txt[g3],murphy.txt[g3]

    This example prints three copies of the file MURPHY.TXT in user 3
    of drive B.

    2B>print *.txt,example.ltr[g0],a:backup.sub[g0s]

    In the above example,  PRINT  prints all the files in the current
    directory with a filetype of TXT, the file EXAMPLE.LTR in user 0,
    and the SUBMIT file  BACKUP.SUB,  which has the SYS attribute and
    is stored in user 0 of drive A.

    10A>print abacus.del[g3],*.vrf[t10] angst.sub[sg0] milton.dun

    The above  command causes  PRINT to print the file  ABACUS.DEL in
    user 3,  and all files  with a  filetype  of  VRF in  the current
    directory.   PRINT  expands the tabs in these files to 10 spaces.
    PRINT then continues with  ANGST.SUB,  a SUBMIT file with the SYS
    attribute in user 0, and finishes with the file MILTON.DUN.  Note
    that commas or spaces are acceptable in separating files.

    B>print *.*

    The above command causes PRINT to print all of the files with the
    DIR attribute in user 0 of drive B.


///1PRINTER
    Syntax:

    PRINTER {n}

    Explanation:

    PRINTER displays or selects the printer device  attached  to  the
    current  virtual  console. Several  consoles  can  share the same
    printer, but only one process can use a given printer at a  time.
    When  you  enter the PRINTER command without a number, the system
    returns the number of the printer assigned to the current virtual
    console. To  select a printer, enter the PRINTER command followed
    by the  printer  number  to  be  attached.  Note:  Refer  to  the
    instruction  manual  supplied  by  your  hardware manufacturer to
    determine how many printers your system supports.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>PRINTER            ; displays the current printer number
    Printer Number = 1

    A>PRINTER 3          ; sets the current printer number
    Printer Number = 3

///1PUT
    Syntax:

    PUT [outfile{,A}{,E|EO}] command-line

    Explanation:

    PUT writes everything a program sends to the console (BDOS
    functions 2, 6 and 9) to a disk file instead of showing it.  The
    program and any programs it starts on this virtual console are
    captured; other consoles are not affected.  An existing outfile is
    replaced.

    A       appends to outfile instead of replacing it.
    E or EO also shows the output on the screen.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected; use
    the program forms such as SDIR.CMD or TYPE.CMD.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>PUT [SDIR.TXT] SDIR.CMD
    A>PUT [BUILD.LOG,A] LINK86 MYPROG
    A>PUT [BUILD.LOG,A,EO] LIB86 MYLIB

    See also: GET, RDR

///1RDR
    Syntax:

    RDR [infile,outfile{,A}{,E|EI|EO}] command-line

    Explanation:

    RDR redirects the console input and the console output of a program
    at the same time: input is read from infile (as with GET) and
    output is written to outfile (as with PUT).

    A       appends to outfile instead of replacing it.
    EI      echoes the input read from infile to the screen.
    EO      echoes the output written to outfile to the screen.
    E       echoes both.

    The command-line must name a program (a .CMD file).  Commands built
    into the shell, such as DIR, TYPE and CLS, are not redirected.
    DOS programs (.COM, .EXE) run by PC-MODE cannot be redirected.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>RDR [SCRIPT.IN,BUILD.LOG] ED MYFILE.TXT
    A>RDR [COMMANDS.DAT,PIP.LOG,EO] PIP
    A>RDR [NEXTCMD.IN,MASTER.LOG,A,EO] PIP

    See also: GET, PUT

///1REN
    Syntax:

    REN {d:}newname{.typ}=oldname{.typ}

    Explanation:

    REN changes the name of the existing file (specified by  oldname)
    to  a  new  name  (specified  by newname). You cannot specify two
    different drives.  If  the  file  specified by newname is already
    present in  the directory,  REN displays the following message on
    the screen:

    Not renamed: filename.ext already exists, delete (Y/N)?

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>REN NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT
    A>REN B:NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT
    A>REN B:NEWLIST=OLDLIST
    A>REN NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT

    Not renamed: NEWFILE.DAT already exists, delete (Y/N)? Y
    NEWFILE.DAT=OLDFILE.DAT

    In the last example, the new filename already  exists.  The  user
    overrides the error by entering a Y at the prompt.


///1SDIR
    Syntax:

    SDIR {d:}{filespec}{,filespec}{[option]|[option=modifier]}

    The SDIR utility is an enhanced version of the DIR utility.  SDIR
    can  search  for  files  on any or all drives, in any or all user
    areas. Only one option list is allowed. The most efficient way to
    become  familiar with SDIR is to use it. SDIR does not change any
    information on diskette or in memory, so you can experiment  with
    it freely.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>SDIR [xfcb] D:*.CMD
    A>SDIR [nosort,ro] A: B: C:
    A>SDIR [user=3,exclude] *.CMD
    A>SDIR [size,rw] D:
    A>SDIR B: [PAGE]
    A>SDIR [PAGE,FULL] A: B:
    A>SDIR [user=all,drive=all,sys] *.PLI *.CMD *.A86

    ///2OPTIONS
    Options:

    [DRIVE=d]     Displays files on the specified drive only.
    [DRIVE=all]   Displays files on all online drives.
    [EXCLUDE]     Displays only files that do not match filespec.
    [FF]          Prints a Form Feed character at the start of each header.
    [FULL]        The default SDIR option.
    [SHORT]       Displays names only, with a backslash for DOS directories.
    [HELP]        Displays a compact summary of the supported options.
    [LENGTH=n]    Displays a new (Page) header every n lines.
    [MESSAGE]     Causes SDIR to display where it is searching for files.
    [SORT]        Sorts names and types (the default).
    [ATTRIBUTES]  Displays archive and user-defined attribute bits.
    [DATE]        Displays timestamps where timestamp information exists.
    [NOSORT]      Displays files in the order that they are found on the disk.
    [PAGE]        Pauses after 23 screen rows, including headers and wraps.
    [NOPAGE]      Disables pauses (the default).
    [RO]          Displays Read-Only files only.
    [RW]          Displays Read/Write attribute files only.
    [SIZE]        Displays only file name and size.
    [SYS]         Displays SYSTEM or FAT HIDDEN files only.
    [DIR]         Displays non-system files (not a subdirectory-only filter).
    [USER=n]      Displays files on user n only.
    [USER=all]    Displays files on all user numbers.
    [XFCB]        Displays only files with XFCBs or date stamping.
    [NONXFCB]     Displays only files without XFCBs or date stamping.

    On CDOS, DOS subdirectories display <DIR> instead of a file size in FULL/SIZE;
    SHORT/SIZE prefix directory names with a backslash. Directory counts
    are separate from regular files. SYSTEM/HIDDEN require [SYS] or [DIR SYS].

    Default SDIR shows normal files only on CP/M and FAT.
    [DIR SYS] shows all files. FULL Type: Sys=SYSTEM, Hid=HIDDEN,
    S+H=both. SHORT/SIZE retain compact output with the same filtering.

    [PAGE] uses the native PCTMP message: Press any key to continue...
    Press a key for the next page, or Ctrl-C to stop. [LENGTH=n] controls
    repeated headers separately; it does not enable interactive pauses.


///1SET
SET filespec [RO|RW,DIR|SYS,ARCHIVE=ON|OFF,F1=ON|OFF,...,F4=ON|OFF]
SET filespec [HIDDEN=ON|OFF]
SET filespec[;password] [PASSWORD=newpassword,PROTECT=READ|WRITE|DELETE|NONE]
SET {d:} [NAME=label.typ]
SET {d:} [RO|RW]
SET {d:} [PROTECT=ON|OFF]
SET {d:} [DEFAULT=password]
SET {d:} [CREATE=ON|OFF,ACCESS=ON|OFF,UPDATE=ON|OFF]
SET [HELP]

File attributes work on CP/M and, on CDOS, DOS media through BDOS. F1-F4 are CP/M
file flags. FAT supports legacy F1-F3 only: its lowercase-extension flag
is not F4, and SET leaves FAT case flags unchanged. FAT short names match
without case. CP/M ARCHIVE=ON clears DOS's archive-needed bit; OFF sets it.

On CDOS FAT, SET d: [NAME=WORK] creates or changes the volume name.
SET d: [NAME="MY DISK"] accepts spaces, up to eleven characters.
SET d: [NAME=] removes the FAT label. The physical root is used even
from a subdirectory; ordinary files with the same name are unaffected.
CP/M directory-label behavior is unchanged.

HIDDEN=ON/OFF changes only the FAT hidden attribute. Wildcards and other
file options may be combined. Requires CDOS DOS media and the BDOS 230
capability handshake; older kernels are rejected. DIR/SDIR visibility and
ERA/RM behavior are unchanged.

XFCB passwords and timestamp settings require CP/M media. Enable
drive protection before assigning passwords or a default password:
  SET A: [PROTECT=ON]
  SET A:*.TXT [PASSWORD=SECRET,PROTECT=READ]

READ protects reading, writing, renaming and deletion; WRITE protects
writing, renaming and deletion; DELETE protects renaming and deletion;
NONE removes file protection. The default password is inherited by
later commands. Password input supports Backspace, Ctrl-X and Ctrl-C.

To clear passwords, use an empty value followed by another option:
  SET A:FILE.TXT [PASSWORD=,PROTECT=NONE]
  SET A: [PASSWORD=,PROTECT=OFF]
[PASSWORD=] alone is rejected by the historical parser.

CREATE and ACCESS timestamps are mutually exclusive. Enabling either
replaces the other; selecting both together is invalid. INITDIR must
format the directory before timestamping can be enabled. UPDATE is
independent. A bare filespec is not a query: use SDIR or SHOW to inspect.


///1SETUP
    Syntax:

    SETUP

    SETUP is a menu-driven  utility program that allows you to tailor
    your  system  configuration  to meet your needs.   SETUP works by
    presenting  you  with  menus.   You  respond to SETUP by pressing
    function  keys  appropriate  to  your  choices.  SETUP offers the
    following options:

    o Save XIOS Parameters - Allows  you  to  save  the  serial  port
      configurations you make with CONFIG.   Also  allows you to save
      the  current  function  key  assignments made with the FUNCTION
      command.

    o MDISK - This  option  allows the user to create a virtual drive
      in memory.

    o Diskette Head Step Time - Allows you  to  change  the head step
      time in the diskette controller.

    o Verify after Diskette Write - When enabled,  this option causes
      Concurrent CP/M  to  verify  each  write operation to diskette,
      with automatic retries for verification errors.

    o Storage Disk - Allows you to  choose  where the  selections you
      make with SETUP are stored.   If you boot  Concurrent CP/M from
      diskette, select "Diskette"; if you boot from hard disk, select
      "Hard Disk".

    When you type SETUP and press Enter, SETUP responds with its main
    menu,offering you all of the above options. Press the appropriate
    function key to receive an option menu. Each option menu explains
    the option  and  offers  you the choice of enabling the option or
    returning to the main menu.

    From the main menu, you have the choice of leaving without making
    changes,  or enabling the changes made in the option menus.   The
    changes made in SETUP do not take  effect until the next time you
    power up your Personal Computer or perform a system reset.

///1SHOW
    Syntax:

    SHOW {d:}{option}

    SHOW by itself displays the drive, the Read Only  or  Read  Write
    mode  for the optionally specified drive, and the remaining space
    in kilobytes for all logged-in drives in the system.

    Use the SHOW options to  display  drive  characteristics,  active
    user  numbers,  or the directory label. SHOW HELP displays a list
    of available options. SPACE and DRIVE work on CP/M media and, on CDOS, DOS media.
    On CDOS FAT, LABEL displays the physical-root volume name, or no label.
    CP/M LABEL retains its password/stamping columns. USERS requires CP/M media.
    On CDOS, default SPACE listings omit N:/O:/P:, as in the OEM command.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    SHOW
    SHOW SPACE
    SHOW DRIVES
    SHOW USERS
    SHOW LABEL
    SHOW HELP
    SHOW A:SPACE
    SHOW B:DRIVE
    SHOW C:USERS
    SHOW D:LABEL

    ///2OPTIONS
    Options:

    SHOW SPACE    ; Same as the SHOW display.

    SHOW DRIVES   ; Displays the drive characteristics of logged-in
                  ; drives on the system, or for a specified drive.

    SHOW USERS    ; Displays the current user number and all user areas
                  ; on the drive that have files assigned to them.

    SHOW LABEL    ; Returns a display of the optional directory label,
                  ; if it has been created.

    SHOW HELP     ; Displays a list of the SHOW options.

    SHOW d:       ; SHOW with the optional drive specifier displays
                  ; information for the specified drive only.


///1SUBMIT
    Syntax:

    SUBMIT filespec (optional parameters)

    Explanation:

    SUBMIT  processes  a  command  file  with  a  filetype   of  .SUB
    consisting  of  Concurrent  CP/M  commands  given one command per
    line.   Any  optional  parameters  (such  as a drive or filespec)
    following  the  filespec  in the command line are substituted for
    their corresponding formal parameters ($1,$2,$3...) in the SUBMIT
    file.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>SUBMIT START
    A>SUBMIT B:START
    A>SUBMIT MAX.SUB data1 data2 data3


///1SETDEF
    Syntax:

    SETDEF
    SETDEF drive-list
    SETDEF [ORDER=(CMD)]
    SETDEF [ORDER=(CMD,SUB)]
    SETDEF [ORDER=(CMD,DOS)]
    SETDEF [ORDER=(CMD,SUB,DOS)]
    SETDEF drive-list [ORDER=(CMD,SUB,DOS)]

    Display or change command search settings for this console. With no
    arguments, SETDEF displays the drive list and file order. Defaults
    are * (current drive) and CMD,DOS. Sys Disk is shown last in the
    drive list as a fixed role. Path and order are independent:
    changing either preserves the other. Invalid input changes neither.

    A list has one to four comma-separated entries: * for the current
    drive, or A: through P:. Spaces/tabs around entries are allowed.

    SETDEF *,A:,C:                 Search current drive, A:, then C:.
    SETDEF M:,*                    Prefer utilities copied to M:.
    SETDEF [ORDER=(CMD,SUB)]        Enable automatic SUB scripts.
    SETDEF * [ORDER=(CMD,DOS)]      Restore both defaults.

    Four orders are accepted:
      CMD                 CMD only; no automatic SUB or DOS lookup.
      CMD,SUB             CMD then SUB; no automatic DOS lookup.
      CMD,DOS             CMD then COM, EXE, BAT (default).
      CMD,SUB,DOS         CMD then SUB, COM, EXE, BAT.

    DOS is a fixed group: COM then EXE then BAT. Native TMP ignores DOS
    and uses only CMD/SUB. The syntax and stored settings are the same
    in native TMP and PC-MODE. Native TMP cannot run DOS programs.
    SUB alone, SUB,CMD and individual COM/EXE/BAT entries are rejected.

    Try the selected types on each drive before moving to the next.
    A SUB or COM on an earlier drive can precede a CMD on a later drive.
    An explicit extension bypasses ORDER: .SUB selects a script even
    when automatic SUB lookup is disabled; in PC-MODE an explicit .COM,
    .EXE or .BAT works even when automatic DOS lookup is disabled.
    Native TMP accepts only .CMD and .SUB. An existing invalid CMD
    reports its load error without trying another type or drive.
    BAT uses PC-MODE's existing BATCH route and needs its interpreter;
    enabling DOS does not install a BAT interpreter.

    Resident commands have priority for names without an explicit type.
    An explicit drive bypasses the list. Missing drives are skipped and
    duplicates searched once. Your working drive and user are preserved.

    The system drive is the final fallback, with existing SYS-file
    rules for CMD and SUB. If also in the normal list, it is searched
    there normally and not again. SYSDISK changes that drive globally;
    SETDEF settings are local to this console and last until reboot.

    Automatic SUB execution requires the matching rebuilt SUBMIT.CMD,
    searched as CMD only through the same path and system fallback.
    SUBMIT.CMD not found means the script did not run. An oversized
    expanded tail reports Command tail too long for SUBMIT; arguments
    are never truncated. The expanded tail (qualified script name,
    optional password and arguments) can contain at most 126 characters.

    SUBMIT's parameter/quoting rules are unchanged. Commands inside
    scripts and program chaining use these settings. An explicit SUBMIT
    script command retains SUBMIT's current/system script lookup.

    Put SETDEF in STARTUP.nnn or a startup SUBMIT script to configure a
    console automatically. A:SETDEF specifies the utility's own drive.
    M: must already exist and contain the utilities to run.
    SETDEF does not allocate, format or copy files to the memory disk.
    TEMPORARY and DISPLAY options are not implemented.

///1SYSDISK
    Syntax:

    SYSDISK
    SYSDISK d:

    Explanation:

    SYSDISK is available as a TMP built-in and as SYSDISK.CMD. The bare
    command uses the built-in; SYSDISK.CMD selects the standalone tool.
    A change is refused while files are open anywhere in the system.

    The SYSDISK command  tells you which  drive is the current system
    drive  and  lets  you  specify  a  new system drive.  Valid drive
    specifications are A: through P:, provided the drive is configured.
    The distributed floppy setup provides A: and B:; other drives such as
    M: require a system configuration that enables them.

    Concurrent CP/M  assigns  one  drive as the system drive whenever
    you start up Concurrent CP/M.  Concurrent CP/M  looks for program
    files (.CMD filetype) on the system drive when it cannot find the
    file using the SETDEF command drive search list. The system drive
    is initially the drive whose letter appears in the system prompt
    when you start up Concurrent CP/M.

    SYSDISK is especially useful if you do not have a hard disk.  You
    can create a start-up file with a SUBMIT command  that sets up an
    MDISK (when configured), sets your system drive to drive M, and transfers  all your
    command files to drive M.  This  frees  up  your  default  system
    drive for other work.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    Here the SYSDISK command is used to tell you which is the current
    system drive:

    A>SYSDISK

    System disk is A:


    In the following example, SYSDISK is used to  assign  the  system
    drive to MDISK, drive M.

    A>SYSDISK M:

    System disk is M:

///1systat
    Syntax:

    SYSTAT
    SYSTAT {[option]}
    SYSTAT {[option {C} {n}]}

    Purpose:

    The  SYSTAT  utility shows the internal state of Concurrent CP/M-
    86.  It is useful for  program  and  system  development.  SYSTAT
    displays  memory  allocation,  current  processes,  system  queue
    activity,  and  many  parameters  associated  with  system   data
    structures. SYSTAT  can  present  either  a  static  picture or a
    continuous, real-time display of these system parameters.

    ///2examples
    Examples:

          A>SYSTAT

    This command invokes  the  menu-driven  feature  of  SYSTAT.  The
    utility responds by displaying the following menu:

          Which Option ?

             H(elp)
             M(emory)
             O(verview)
             P(rocesses - All)
             Q(ueues)
             U(ser Processes)
             C(onsoles)
             E(xit)

          ->_


    Typing the appropriate letter in response to the menu obtains the
    associated  display. To  use SYSTAT without the menu, specify the
    menu option letter in the command, like this:

          A>SYSTAT [O] 	       ; this command displays a snapshot
                               ; overview of the system.
          A>SYSTAT [M C]       ; this command displays a continuous
                               ; picture of memory allocation.
          A>SYSTAT [U C 5]     ; this command displays snapshots
                               ; of the user processes every 5 seconds.

    ///2options
    [H]     Displays help for SYSTAT command.

    [M]     Displays all memory potentially available to  users,  but
            does  not  display restricted memory.  The partitions are
            listed in memory-address order.

    [O]     Displays an overview of the system generation parameters.

    [P]     Displays all system processes and resources they use.

    [Q]     Displays  all  queues  and  their  readers,  writers  and
            owners.

    [U]     Displays only user-initiated processes (similar to  [P]).

    [C]     Displays physical console number, virtual console number,
            the name of the process attached to the console, whether
            it is a  foreground  background console, and whether the
            console is in dynamic or buffered mode.

    [E]     Returns you to the system prompt level from the menu.

    [option C n]
            Following  an  option  with a space  and a  C activates a
            continuous display, until you press a key.  Following the
            C with a space and a one  or two-digit  number causes the
            display to update every n seconds.


///1TYPE
    Syntax:

    TYPE filespec
    TYPE filespec [PAGE]
    TYPE filespec [PNN]

    Explanation:

    TYPE displays an ASCII text file up to its CP/M Ctrl-Z end marker.
    CP/M files end at Ctrl-Z or a record boundary; on CDOS, DOS files stop
    at their exact byte length, avoiding final-record padding. A last
    line does not need CR/LF. Wildcards are not accepted.
    [PAGE] pauses after 23 screen rows,
    counting tabs and wrapped text. Paging is off by default.

    The prompt is the native PCTMP PAUSE message:
    Press any key to continue...
    Press a key for the next page, or Ctrl-C to stop. A short file or a
    file ending exactly at the page boundary needs no extra keypress.
    [PNN] selects a smaller page: NN must be two digits, 01 through 23.
    Example: TYPE notes.txt [P10]. [PAGE] uses 23 physical rows.
    Unbracketed P/PNN, [P], [P00] and [P24] are invalid.
    A password can be supplied as filespec;password, or entered when
    prompted. Password entry accepts Backspace, Ctrl-X and Ctrl-C.

    Entering ^P before TYPE echoes output to the printer until the next ^P.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>TYPE letter.dat
    B>TYPE a:document.law [PAGE]
    C>TYPE program.bas [PAGE]
    D>TYPE program.a86


///1USER
    Syntax:

    USER {number 0 - 15}

    Explanation:

    USER displays and changes the current user number.  USER with  no
    command  tail  displays  the  current  user  number.  USER with a
    number from 0 to 15 changes the current user number to the number
    specified  by n.  CP/M assumes a default user number of 0.  Files
    set to SYStem on USER 0 are available to all USER areas as  Read-
    Only.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>USER
    B>USER 0
    A>USER 15
    A>USER 7

///1VCMODE
    Syntax:

    VCMODE {option}

    VCMODE specifies background operating modes for the four  virtual
    consoles. When  a virtual console is switched-out, it operates in
    either the dynamic mode or the buffered mode. See the  HELP  file
    explanations under BUFFERED  and DYNAMIC  for more information.

    ///2EXAMPLES
    Examples:

    A>VCMODE
    A>VCMODE dynamic
    A>VCMODE buffered
    A>VCMODE size=5

    ///2OPTIONS
    Options:

    DYNAMIC  switches the console to dynamic mode. If a console is in
             dynamic mode and you  switch to a different console, you
             will not see any data that  may have scrolled  off  it's
             screen while you were switched away.

    BUFFERED If a console is in buffered mode and switched out,  data
             output from a running program goes into a buffer file on
             disk.  Thus screen output is preserved.

///1WINDOW
WINDOW VIEW
WINDOW TOP N=n
WINDOW FULL N=n
WINDOW CHANGE N=n PR=r,PC=c,NR=rows,NC=cols,VR=r,VC=c,TR=ROW,D=COLOR
WINDOW WRITE N=n TYPE=WINDOW|CONSOLE|SETUP,FILE=filespec

Window numbers are 0..3 on CCP/M-86 and 1..4 on CDOS. BDOS version
selects the range at runtime. Screen/virtual coordinates are one-based:
rows 1..24, columns 1..80. CHANGE validates its fields before changing XIOS;
extents are clipped to the screen. VIEW reports all windows; TOP brings a
window forward; FULL expands it to the screen.

CHANGE fields (abbreviations allowed):
  NUMBER/N       Window number: CCP/M 0..3, CDOS 1..4
  PROW/PR        Physical top row
  PCOL/PC        Physical left column
  NROWS/NR       Window height
  NCOLS/NC       Window width
  VROW/VR        First visible row in the virtual console
  VCOL/VC        First visible column in the virtual console
  TRACKING/TR    NO or ROW cursor tracking
  DISPLAY/D      B&W or COLOR
  FGCOLOR/FG     Foreground color
  BGCOLOR/BG     Background color
Colors: BLACK BLUE GREEN CYAN RED MAGENTA YELLOW WHITE.

WRITE WINDOW saves the visible virtual-screen rectangle. CONSOLE saves
all 24 rows. Both trim trailing spaces. SETUP saves command lines for
all four windows, even though a valid N is still required. Output is
text ending at Ctrl-Z; disk-record padding is not part of the text.

  WINDOW CH N=1 PR=2,PC=4,NR=5,NC=30,FG=WHITE,BG=BLUE
  WINDOW WRITE N=1 TYPE=SETUP,FILE=B:WINDOWS.SUB

WINDOW does not require WMENU to be installed. The resident manager is
a separate interactive interface to the same window services.


///1WMENU
WMENU
Installs and detaches the resident window manager. Once installed,
Ctrl-Alt-Esc or Ctrl-Alt-0 opens its menu; Ctrl-keypad-Plus also works.

Arrows or Space select a highlighted item; Return opens it:
  Place     Move the window with arrows
  Size      Change its dimensions with arrows
  Scroll    Change its view into the virtual console
  Tracking  Toggle NO/ROW with Up/Down or Space
  Display   Toggle B&W/COLOR with Up/Down or Space
  Color     Left/Right selects foreground/background; Up/Down changes it
  Write     Save Window, Console or Setup output
  Abort     Return confirms unloading; Esc returns to the menu

Window numbers are 0..3 on CCP/M-86 and 1..4 on CDOS. Esc or Return leaves an editing submenu with
live changes in effect. Esc from the main menu hides it without unloading.
Write uses a default d:WINDOWn.TXT filename; Return writes, Esc cancels.
Ctrl-Alt-1..4 and Ctrl-Alt-Tab remain the console-switch shortcuts.
WINDOW provides the nonresident command-line interface.


///1CHKCPMFS
CHKCPMFS drive: [FV]
CP/M directory allocation check on the specified A:..P:. Exactly one drive
is required; options follow it. V lists checked entries. All users 0-15 are
checked. Without F, no disk writes are made.
F offers confirmed metadata repairs only: clear reserved extent bits and
remove fully identical duplicate extents with matching timestamp information.
File data, allocation maps, names, attributes and recorded lengths are retained.
Conflicting extents, cross-links, invalid block references, record-count errors
or ambiguous associated metadata prevent the whole repair plan.
Run on an idle drive. Repairs use BIOS on CP/M and locked XIOS on CCP/M/CDOS.
I/O failures stop writes; partial changes are reported. DOS/FAT is rejected.

///1ASM80
ASM80 basename{.shp}

Assembles basename.ASM as Intel 8080 source and produces basename.HEX
and basename.PRN. With no suffix, source and output use the current drive.
The optional three-letter suffix selects source, HEX and listing drives;
use Z for either output to suppress it, or X for a console listing.

  ASM80 PROG
  ASM80 PROG.AAZ

The second example reads A:PROG.ASM, writes A:PROG.HEX and suppresses
PROG.PRN. This is the translated CP/M-80 assembler, not the native 8086
ASM86 assembler. HEXCOM converts its Intel HEX output to a flat COM image.


///1HEXCOM
HEXCOM basename

Reads basename.HEX and writes basename.COM. A supplied .HEX extension
is also accepted. The output is a flat memory image from the lowest to
the highest loaded address, padded to a 128-byte CP/M record with Ctrl-Z.

  HEXCOM PROG
  HEXCOM B:PROG.HEX

This is a development tool for Intel HEX input. Use GENCMD, not HEXCOM,
to package native CP/M-86 segmented H86 output as a CMD program.

